<article>
<h1>Creating New Blocks</h1>
<p class="why">Why this matters: every new hardware device your team tests against needs a block — knowing both creation paths means you can add a block in 5 minutes (JSON) or 45 minutes (Python) depending on how much simulation fidelity you need.</p>

<!-- ══ §0 TWO PATHS ══ -->
<section id="s0">
<h2><span class="sn">§0</span> Two paths to a new block <button class="db" onclick="tick(0,this)">Mark done</button></h2>

<p>You already know the fast path from lesson 0006 (JSON spec). Here is where both paths lead and when to choose each:</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Capability</th><th>JSON spec (5 min)</th><th>Python class (45 min)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>Stateful simulation (remember VOLT, return it on query)</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Computed/noisy measurements</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Custom block face (live meter, waveform preview)</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Right-click shortcuts</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>No Python required</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Works without repo change (user dir)</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
</table>

<p><strong>Rule of thumb:</strong> start with a JSON spec to verify that your firmware sends the right command structure. Promote to a Python class when you need the device to remember state and compute realistic responses.</p>

<p>A Python block is three files and one registration line:</p>
<div class="fmap">
<span class="dir">silmulator/</span>
├── <span class="dir">devices/</span>
│   └── <span class="new">my_device.py</span>        <span class="cm">← Step 1: simulation logic (BaseDevice subclass)</span>
└── <span class="dir">gui/blocks/</span>
    └── <span class="new">my_block.py</span>         <span class="cm">← Step 2: visual widget (BaseBlock subclass)</span>

<span class="dir">silmulator/gui/canvas.py</span>   <span class="cm">← Step 3: import + add to BLOCK_REGISTRY</span>
</div>
</section>

<!-- ══ §1 DEVICE LAYER ══ -->
<section id="s1">
<h2><span class="sn">§1</span> Step 1 — The device layer <button class="db" onclick="tick(1,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fp">silmulator/devices/my_device.py  (new file)</div>
<div class="fp">Reference: silmulator/devices/power_supply.py</div>

<p>The device is pure simulation logic — no Qt, no GUI. It inherits from <code>BaseDevice</code> and must implement two methods: <code>_setup_handlers()</code> and <code>_do_reset()</code>.</p>

<pre>
<span class="kw">from</span> silmulator.devices.base_device <span class="kw">import</span> BaseDevice, ScpiError, ScpiCommand
<span class="kw">from</span> dataclasses <span class="kw">import</span> dataclass

<span class="at">@dataclass</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">MyState</span>:                    <span class="cm">← optional: bundle state into a dataclass</span>
    voltage: float = <span class="num">0.0</span>
    output_on: bool = <span class="op">False</span>

<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">MyDevice</span>(<span class="cls">BaseDevice</span>):
    MANUFACTURER = <span class="str">"Acme"</span>         <span class="cm">← returned by *IDN? automatically</span>
    MODEL        = <span class="str">"AX-1000"</span>
    SERIAL       = <span class="str">"SIM000001"</span>
    FIRMWARE     = <span class="str">"1.0.0"</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        self.state = <span class="cls">MyState</span>()     <span class="cm">← initialise state BEFORE super().__init__()</span>
        <span class="kw">super</span>().__init__()           <span class="cm">← calls _setup_handlers() and _setup_common_handlers()</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_setup_handlers</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        self.<span class="fn">_register</span>(<span class="str">"VOLT"</span>,  self.<span class="fn">_volt</span>)   <span class="cm">← register command → handler</span>
        self.<span class="fn">_register</span>(<span class="str">"OUTP"</span>,  self.<span class="fn">_outp</span>)
        self.<span class="fn">_register</span>(<span class="str">"MEAS:VOLT"</span>, self.<span class="fn">_meas_volt</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_volt</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> <span class="cls">str</span>:
        <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.is_query:                          <span class="cm">← VOLT? → return set value</span>
            <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">f</span><span class="str">"{self.state.voltage:.4f}"</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.params:                            <span class="cm">← VOLT 5.0 → store it</span>
            v = <span class="fn">float</span>(cmd.params[<span class="num">0</span>])
            <span class="kw">if</span> v < <span class="num">0</span> <span class="kw">or</span> v > <span class="num">32</span>:
                <span class="kw">raise</span> <span class="cls">ScpiError</span>(-<span class="num">222</span>, <span class="str">"Data out of range"</span>)
            self.state.voltage = v
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">""</span>                                 <span class="cm">← writes return empty string</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_outp</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> <span class="cls">str</span>:
        <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.is_query:
            <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">"1"</span> <span class="kw">if</span> self.state.output_on <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="str">"0"</span>
        <span class="kw">if</span> cmd.params:
            self.state.output_on = cmd.params[<span class="num">0</span>].upper() <span class="kw">in</span> (<span class="str">"1"</span>, <span class="str">"ON"</span>)
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="str">""</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_meas_volt</span>(self, cmd: <span class="cls">ScpiCommand</span>) -> <span class="cls">str</span>:
        <span class="cm">← queries return measured value + noise</span>
        measured = self.state.voltage <span class="kw">if</span> self.state.output_on <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="num">0.0</span>
        noise = measured * <span class="num">1e-4</span>              <span class="cm">← ≈ 0.01% Gaussian noise</span>
        <span class="kw">import</span> random
        <span class="kw">return</span> <span class="fn">f</span><span class="str">"{measured + random.gauss(0, noise):.6f}"</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_do_reset</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        self.state = <span class="cls">MyState</span>()              <span class="cm">← restore factory defaults</span>
</pre>

<h3>Three rules for handler functions</h3>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Writes always return <code>""</code></strong> — any other non-empty string would be echoed back to the caller as if it were a query response.</li>
  <li><strong>Queries return a string</strong> — even numeric values must be returned as <code>str(value)</code> because <code>BaseDevice.process()</code> joins responses with <code>"\n".join(responses)</code>.</li>
  <li><strong>Invalid input raises <code>ScpiError</code></strong> — <code>BaseDevice._dispatch()</code> catches it and pushes the error code into the error queue (reachable via <code>SYSTEM:ERROR?</code>). Don't use regular <code>ValueError</code>.</li>
</ul>

<h3>Why state must be initialised before super().__init__()</h3>
<p><code>super().__init__()</code> calls <code>_setup_handlers()</code>, which registers handler methods that reference <code>self.state</code>. If <code>self.state</code> hasn't been created yet, the first call to any handler will raise <code>AttributeError</code>. Always set instance variables before calling <code>super().__init__()</code>.</p>

<h3>_register() and _register_alias()</h3>
<p><code>_register(path, fn)</code> stores <code>fn</code> in <code>self._handlers[path.upper()]</code>. SCPI devices typically support short-form and long-form paths for the same command (e.g. <code>VOLT</code> and <code>SOURCE:VOLTAGE:LEVEL:IMMEDIATE:AMPLITUDE</code>). Register each variant:</p>
<pre>
self.<span class="fn">_register</span>(<span class="str">"VOLT"</span>,                     self.<span class="fn">_volt</span>)
self.<span class="fn">_register</span>(<span class="str">"SOURCE:VOLTAGE"</span>,           self.<span class="fn">_volt</span>)  <span class="cm">← same handler</span>
self.<span class="fn">_register_alias</span>(<span class="str">"SOUR:VOLT"</span>, <span class="str">"VOLT"</span>)               <span class="cm">← alias to existing</span>
</pre>
</section>

<!-- ══ §2 BLOCK LAYER ══ -->
<section id="s2">
<h2><span class="sn">§2</span> Step 2 — The block layer <button class="db" onclick="tick(2,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fp">silmulator/gui/blocks/my_block.py  (new file)</div>
<div class="fp">Reference: silmulator/gui/blocks/multimeter_block.py</div>

<p>The block is the GUI widget. It inherits from <code>BaseBlock</code> and must implement <code>_setup_ports()</code>. Three optional overrides control appearance and behaviour:</p>

<pre>
<span class="kw">from</span> PyQt6.QtCore <span class="kw">import</span> QRectF
<span class="kw">from</span> PyQt6.QtGui  <span class="kw">import</span> QPainter, QColor, QPen, QBrush, QFont
<span class="kw">from</span> PyQt6.QtWidgets <span class="kw">import</span> QMenu
<span class="kw">from</span> silmulator.gui.blocks.base_block <span class="kw">import</span> BaseBlock
<span class="kw">from</span> silmulator.devices.my_device <span class="kw">import</span> MyDevice


<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">MyBlock</span>(<span class="cls">BaseBlock</span>):

    BLOCK_WIDTH  = <span class="num">220</span>             <span class="cm">← canvas width in px</span>
    BLOCK_HEIGHT = <span class="num">160</span>             <span class="cm">← canvas height in px</span>
    HEADER_COLOR = <span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">40</span>, <span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">140</span>)  <span class="cm">← title bar colour</span>
    BODY_COLOR   = <span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">240</span>, <span class="num">245</span>, <span class="num">255</span>)  <span class="cm">← body background</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        <span class="kw">super</span>().__init__(<span class="cls">MyDevice</span>(), <span class="str">"My Block"</span>)
        <span class="cm">#                  ↑ device    ↑ BLOCK_REGISTRY key / display name</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_setup_ports</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"IN"</span>,  <span class="str">"IN"</span>,  <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.5</span>)  <span class="cm">← id, label, x_frac, y_frac</span>
        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"OUT"</span>, <span class="str">"OUT"</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>, <span class="num">0.5</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_paint_content</span>(self, painter: <span class="cls">QPainter</span>, rect: <span class="cls">QRectF</span>) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        <span class="cm">← called by BaseBlock.paint() after the title bar is drawn</span>
        dev = self.device          <span class="cm">← access the device for live readings</span>
        painter.<span class="fn">setFont</span>(<span class="cls">QFont</span>(<span class="str">"Consolas"</span>, <span class="num">10</span>))
        painter.<span class="fn">setPen</span>(<span class="cls">QPen</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">30</span>)))

        <span class="cm"># draw voltage reading below the title bar</span>
        y = self.TITLE_HEIGHT + <span class="num">24</span>
        volt_str = <span class="fn">f</span><span class="str">"{dev.state.voltage:.4f} V"</span>
        painter.<span class="fn">drawText</span>(<span class="fn">int</span>(rect.x() + <span class="num">10</span>), y, volt_str)

        <span class="cm"># output indicator dot</span>
        color = <span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">220</span>, <span class="num">80</span>) <span class="kw">if</span> dev.state.output_on <span class="kw">else</span> <span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">80</span>)
        painter.<span class="fn">setBrush</span>(<span class="cls">QBrush</span>(color))
        painter.<span class="fn">setPen</span>(<span class="cls">QPen</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">20</span>), <span class="num">1</span>))
        painter.<span class="fn">drawEllipse</span>(<span class="fn">int</span>(rect.right() - <span class="num">24</span>), y - <span class="num">12</span>, <span class="num">10</span>, <span class="num">10</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_add_device_menu_actions</span>(self, menu: <span class="cls">QMenu</span>) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        <span class="cm">← adds entries below the built-in "Send SCPI", "Reset", "View log" items</span>
        menu.<span class="fn">addAction</span>(<span class="str">"Set 5V ON"</span>,  <span class="kw">lambda</span>: (
            self.<span class="fn">send_command</span>(<span class="str">"VOLT 5.0"</span>), self.<span class="fn">send_command</span>(<span class="str">"OUTP ON"</span>), self.<span class="fn">update</span>()))
        menu.<span class="fn">addAction</span>(<span class="str">"Output OFF"</span>, <span class="kw">lambda</span>: (
            self.<span class="fn">send_command</span>(<span class="str">"OUTP OFF"</span>), self.<span class="fn">update</span>()))
</pre>

<h3>Four methods you can override</h3>
<div class="steps">
  <span class="sn">_setup_ports()</span>
  <span class="st"><strong>Required.</strong> Called by <code>BaseBlock.__init__</code>. Use <code>add_port(id, label, x, y)</code> to place connection ports.</span>
  <span class="sn">_paint_content()</span>
  <span class="st">Optional. Called by <code>BaseBlock.paint()</code> after the title bar is drawn. Use standard <code>QPainter</code> calls to draw meters, indicators, text. Access live device state via <code>self.device</code>.</span>
  <span class="sn">_add_device_menu_actions()</span>
  <span class="st">Optional. Called by <code>BaseBlock.contextMenuEvent()</code>. Add <code>QAction</code>s for frequently-used command sequences.</span>
  <span class="sn">_do_reset()</span>
  <span class="st">In the <em>device</em>, not the block. Override to restore device-specific state on <code>*RST</code> or right-click → Reset device.</span>
</div>

<div class="note"><strong>self.update()</strong> — call this after any command that changes device state that <code>_paint_content</code> reads. It schedules a repaint. Without it, the block face won't refresh until Qt decides to repaint on its own (which may be never for a stationary block).</div>
</section>

<!-- ══ §3 REGISTRATION ══ -->
<section id="s3">
<h2><span class="sn">§3</span> Step 3 — Registration in canvas.py <button class="db" onclick="tick(3,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fp">silmulator/gui/canvas.py:18–35</div>

<p>Two edits to <code>canvas.py</code>:</p>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># Add the import alongside the other block imports</span>
<span class="kw">from</span> silmulator.gui.blocks.my_block <span class="kw">import</span> MyBlock

<span class="cm"># Add the entry to BLOCK_REGISTRY</span>
BLOCK_REGISTRY = {
    <span class="str">"Oscilloscope"</span>:       OscilloscopeBlock,
    <span class="str">"Power Supply"</span>:       PowerSupplyBlock,
    <span class="str">"Function Generator"</span>: FunctionGeneratorBlock,
    <span class="str">"Multimeter"</span>:         MultimeterBlock,
    <span class="str">"MSP430"</span>:             MSP430Block,
    <span class="str">"Code Input"</span>:         CodeInputBlock,
    <span class="str">"My Block"</span>:           MyBlock,      <span class="cm">← ADD THIS LINE</span>
}
</pre>

<p>The key <code>"My Block"</code> must match the second argument passed to <code>super().__init__()</code> in your block's <code>__init__</code>. It is also the string used in <code>to_dict()</code>/<code>from_dict()</code> — a mismatch causes silent wire loss on reload (the exact same bug as the original "Function Gen" vs "Function Generator" issue).</p>

<h3>Where it appears after registration</h3>
<p>Once the key is in <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY</code>, the block appears automatically in:</p>
<ul>
  <li><strong>Add menu</strong> (menu bar → Add → My Block) — populated from <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY.keys()</code></li>
  <li><strong>SCPI Console target list</strong> — the dropdown shows all blocks currently on canvas</li>
  <li><strong>Save/load round-trips</strong> — <code>to_dict()</code>/<code>from_dict()</code> use the registry key</li>
</ul>
<div class="note">The <code>BlockLibraryPanel</code> (left side panel) has a <em>hardcoded</em> list of descriptions (main_window.py:41–47). A new block will appear in the Add menu but NOT in the library panel unless you also add an entry there. This is a known inconsistency — the panel was written before <code>BLOCK_REGISTRY</code> was made the single source of truth.</div>
</section>

<!-- ══ §4 REAL EXAMPLE ══ -->
<section id="s4">
<h2><span class="sn">§4</span> Real example — MultimeterBlock anatomy <button class="db" onclick="tick(4,this)">Mark done</button></h2>
<div class="fp">silmulator/gui/blocks/multimeter_block.py</div>

<p>The Multimeter is the cleanest production example — less complex than PowerSupply, more complete than a minimal stub.</p>

<h3>Device (Multimeter)</h3>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># devices/multimeter.py — key parts</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">Multimeter</span>(<span class="cls">BaseDevice</span>):
    MANUFACTURER = <span class="str">"HEWLETT-PACKARD"</span>
    MODEL        = <span class="str">"34401A"</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        self.config  = <span class="cls">MeasurementConfig</span>()   <span class="cm">← state first</span>
        self.trigger = <span class="cls">TriggerConfig</span>()
        self.sample  = <span class="cls">SampleConfig</span>()
        self._injected_values: dict = {}       <span class="cm">← inject test values</span>
        <span class="kw">super</span>().__init__()

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_get_reading</span>(self, func: <span class="cls">str</span>) -> <span class="cls">float</span>:
        <span class="cm"># Returns injected value if set, else default + noise</span>
        base = self._injected_values.<span class="fn">get</span>(func, self.DEFAULT_VOLTAGE_DC)
        noise = abs(base) * <span class="num">1e-4</span>
        <span class="kw">return</span> base + random.<span class="fn">gauss</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, noise <span class="kw">or</span> <span class="num">1e-6</span>)
</pre>

<h3>Block (MultimeterBlock)</h3>
<pre>
<span class="cm"># gui/blocks/multimeter_block.py — key parts</span>
<span class="kw">class</span> <span class="cls">MultimeterBlock</span>(<span class="cls">BaseBlock</span>):
    BLOCK_WIDTH  = <span class="num">200</span>
    BLOCK_HEIGHT = <span class="num">160</span>
    HEADER_COLOR = <span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">80</span>, <span class="num">50</span>, <span class="num">120</span>)       <span class="cm">← purple header</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">__init__</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        <span class="kw">super</span>().__init__(<span class="cls">Multimeter</span>(), <span class="str">"Multimeter"</span>)
        self._last_reading: float = <span class="num">0.0</span>        <span class="cm">← block-local display cache</span>

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_setup_ports</span>(self) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"HI"</span>,      <span class="str">"HI"</span>, <span class="num">0.5</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>)  <span class="cm">← bottom edge</span>
        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"LO"</span>,      <span class="str">"LO"</span>, <span class="num">0.7</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>)
        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"CURR_IN"</span>, <span class="str">"A"</span>,  <span class="num">0.3</span>, <span class="num">1.0</span>)
        self.<span class="fn">add_port</span>(<span class="str">"GUARD"</span>,   <span class="str">"G"</span>,  <span class="num">0.0</span>, <span class="num">0.7</span>)

    <span class="kw">def</span> <span class="fn">_paint_content</span>(self, painter, rect) -> <span class="cls">None</span>:
        <span class="cm"># Green-on-black LCD display</span>
        disp_rect = <span class="cls">QRectF</span>(rect.x()+<span class="num">10</span>, self.TITLE_HEIGHT+<span class="num">8</span>, rect.width()-<span class="num">20</span>, <span class="num">70</span>)
        painter.<span class="fn">setBrush</span>(<span class="cls">QBrush</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">30</span>, <span class="num">0</span>)))
        painter.<span class="fn">drawRect</span>(disp_rect)
        <span class="cm"># large green readout</span>
        painter.<span class="fn">setFont</span>(<span class="cls">QFont</span>(<span class="str">"Consolas"</span>, <span class="num">20</span>, <span class="cls">QFont.Weight</span>.Bold))
        painter.<span class="fn">setPen</span>(<span class="cls">QPen</span>(<span class="cls">QColor</span>(<span class="num">0</span>, <span class="num">255</span>, <span class="num">60</span>)))
        painter.<span class="fn">drawText</span>(disp_rect, Qt.AlignmentFlag.AlignCenter, <span class="str">"5.0000 V"</span>)
</pre>

<p>Notice the block-local <code>self._last_reading</code>: this is <em>not</em> device state. It's the last value the block read for display purposes — separate from what the device actually tracks. Blocks can store display caches separately from device state.</p>
</section>

<!-- ══ §5 CONTRAST ══ -->
<section id="s5">
<h2><span class="sn">§5</span> Often confused: device state vs block state <button class="db" onclick="tick(5,this)">Mark done</button></h2>

<p><code>BaseBlock</code> and <code>BaseDevice</code> both live on the canvas. What belongs where?</p>

<table>
  <tr><th>Belongs in…</th><th>Device (BaseDevice subclass)</th><th>Block (BaseBlock subclass)</th></tr>
  <tr><td>SCPI command handlers</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Set voltage, output state, calibration data</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Measurement noise computation</td><td class="yes">✓</td><td class="no">✗</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Display cache (<code>_last_reading</code>)</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>BLOCK_WIDTH / BLOCK_HEIGHT / colours</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Right-click menu shortcuts</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
  <tr><td>Port layout</td><td class="no">✗</td><td class="yes">✓</td></tr>
</table>

<p>The dividing line: <strong>the device must work correctly with no Qt installed</strong>. You can unit-test <code>MyDevice</code> with <code>pytest</code> and no GUI. The block is untestable without a <code>QApplication</code>. Keep device logic free of Qt imports.</p>

<div class="warn">Putting GUI state (e.g. <code>self._last_reading</code>) in the device, or SCPI state (e.g. <code>self.voltage</code>) in the block, breaks this separation and makes the device ununit-testable. The <code>MultimeterBlock._last_reading</code> is a display cache; the "true" voltage reading lives in <code>Multimeter._injected_values</code> and <code>MeasurementConfig</code>.</div>
</section>

<!-- ══ §6 PRACTICE ══ -->
<section id="s6">
<h2><span class="sn">§6</span> Practice <button class="db" onclick="tick(6,this)">Mark done</button></h2>

<div class="quiz">
<h3>Quick-fire quiz</h3>

<!-- Q1 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="1">
<div class="qt">Q1. You call <code>super().__init__()</code> before setting <code>self.state = MyState()</code>. What breaks and when?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="super().__init__() doesn't call _volt() or any handler at construction time — it only registers them. The crash happens on the first SCPI command dispatch, not during __init__.">The constructor crashes immediately with AttributeError because BaseDevice.__init__() calls every handler to test them.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. super().__init__() calls _setup_handlers(), which registers references to self._volt, self._outp, etc. The methods themselves aren't called yet. But the first time process('VOLT?') runs, _volt() executes and reads self.state — which doesn't exist yet — raising AttributeError.">The constructor completes fine, but the first SCPI dispatch crashes with AttributeError because _volt() tries to read self.state which doesn't exist.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="super().__init__() does not call _do_reset(). _do_reset() is only called by the reset() method, triggered by *RST or right-click → Reset device.">super().__init__() calls _do_reset(), which tries to assign self.state = MyState() — but _do_reset() also reads self.state first, causing AttributeError.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="Python attribute access on self doesn't raise NameError — it raises AttributeError. And the crash happens in _volt(), not in _setup_handlers().">Nothing breaks — Python's __init__ methods can be called in any order, and BaseDevice.__init__() doesn't access any attributes before _setup_handlers() is called.</li>
</ul>
<div class="fb" id="fb1"></div>
</div>

<!-- Q2 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="2">
<div class="qt">Q2. A handler for a write command returns <code>"OK"</code> instead of <code>""</code>. What does the caller's firmware see?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="BaseDevice.process() only ignores empty strings. 'OK' is non-empty, so it IS appended to the responses list and returned to the caller.">Nothing — BaseDevice.process() ignores any response from write handlers and only returns query responses.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. BaseDevice._dispatch() returns the handler's return value. process() collects non-empty responses into a list and joins them with newlines. 'OK' is non-empty, so it's included in the return string. The caller's firmware receives 'OK' as if it were a query response.">The caller sees <code>"OK"</code> in the response string — process() joins all non-empty handler return values, whether or not the command was a query.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="ScpiError(-113,...) is raised by _dispatch() only when no handler is found for the path. A handler that returns 'OK' is found and called successfully.">ScpiError(-113) is raised because returning a non-empty string from a write handler violates the SCPI protocol contract.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="Python strings are never silently dropped. BaseDevice.process() explicitly checks 'if resp is not None and resp != \"\"' and appends to the response list.">Nothing — Python silently discards the return value of methods called as side effects.</li>
</ul>
<div class="fb" id="fb2"></div>
</div>

<!-- Q3 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="3">
<div class="qt">Q3. You register your block in BLOCK_REGISTRY as <code>"My PSU"</code> but pass <code>"MyPSU"</code> as the device_type to <code>super().__init__()</code>. A user saves a layout and reloads. What happens?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="BLOCK_REGISTRY.get('MyPSU') returns None because the registry key is 'My PSU', not 'MyPSU'. from_dict() calls add_block(bdata['type']) where bdata['type'] is whatever device_type was stored — 'MyPSU'. This returns None and the block is skipped.">The block loads correctly — BLOCK_REGISTRY looks up 'My PSU' and the device_type mismatch is ignored at load time.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. to_dict() stores device_type = block.device_type = 'MyPSU'. from_dict() calls add_block('MyPSU'). BLOCK_REGISTRY.get('MyPSU') returns None because the registry key is 'My PSU'. The block is silently skipped and all its wires are dropped — the same bug as the original 'Function Gen' vs 'Function Generator' mismatch.">The block and its wires are silently dropped on reload — to_dict() stores 'MyPSU' but BLOCK_REGISTRY has only 'My PSU', so add_block() returns None.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="KeyError is not raised — BLOCK_REGISTRY.get() returns None, not an exception. from_dict() then checks 'if block:' before using it, so None silently drops the block.">A KeyError is raised during from_dict(), alerting the user that the block type doesn't match any registry entry.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="The device_type passed to super().__init__() becomes block.device_type, which is stored in to_dict(). The registry key and device_type must match for round-trips to work.">The block saves and loads fine — BLOCK_REGISTRY key and device_type are independent, and from_dict() matches by class type, not string.</li>
</ul>
<div class="fb" id="fb3"></div>
</div>

<!-- Q4 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="4">
<div class="qt">Q4. After calling <code>send_command("OUTP ON")</code> from a right-click menu action, the block face doesn't update visually. What call is missing?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="repaint() forces synchronous repaint, but self.update() is the preferred call — it schedules an async repaint through Qt's event loop. Both work, but update() is preferred for performance."><code>self.repaint()</code> — only repaint() triggers an immediate visual update; update() only queues a future repaint.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="QApplication.processEvents() drains the event queue but is not the right tool here. Calling it repeatedly can cause recursive event processing and is a code smell. The correct fix is self.update() after the state-changing command."><code>QApplication.processEvents()</code> — the event loop must be pumped manually after state changes from non-Qt code.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. self.update() tells Qt to schedule a repaint of this item. When the event loop runs, Qt calls paint() → _paint_content() which reads the updated device state. Without update(), the block face only repaints if Qt decides to (e.g. when another window overlaps it)."><code>self.update()</code> — schedules a repaint of the QGraphicsItem so _paint_content() is called again with the new device state.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="scene().update() repaints the entire scene, which is wasteful. self.update() is scoped to just this item. Also, _paint_content() is not called directly — it's called by the Qt paint system via BaseBlock.paint()."><code>self.scene().update()</code> then <code>self._paint_content()</code> manually — Qt won't call _paint_content automatically, it must be triggered explicitly.</li>
</ul>
<div class="fb" id="fb4"></div>
</div>

<!-- Q5 -->
<div class="qblock" data-q="5">
<div class="qt">Q5. Your new block appears in the Add menu (menu bar → Add) but NOT in the left-side Hardware Blocks library panel. Why?</div>
<ul class="opts">
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="The Add menu IS populated from BLOCK_REGISTRY dynamically. If the block appears there, it's correctly registered. The issue is specific to the library panel.">The block wasn't added to BLOCK_REGISTRY — the Add menu shows all installed blocks, but the panel uses a separate source.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="discover_specs() is only called for JSON specs in custom_blocks/. Python blocks registered in BLOCK_REGISTRY directly don't go through discover_specs() at all.">discover_specs() hasn't been called since the block was added — the library panel updates on discover_specs() but the Add menu updates on import.</li>
  <li data-correct="true" data-why="Correct. BlockLibraryPanel._setup_ui() (main_window.py:41-54) has a hardcoded 'descriptions' dict with the six original block names. It doesn't iterate BLOCK_REGISTRY. Adding a new Python block to the registry won't update the panel unless you also add an entry to that dict. This is a known inconsistency in the codebase.">BlockLibraryPanel has a hardcoded descriptions dict with the original six blocks. It doesn't read BLOCK_REGISTRY — new blocks must be added there manually too.</li>
  <li data-correct="false" data-why="BLOCK_HEIGHT has no effect on whether a block appears in the library panel. The panel list is built from the hardcoded descriptions dict, not from block geometry.">The block's BLOCK_HEIGHT is too large — blocks taller than 200px are excluded from the library panel to prevent layout overflow.</li>
</ul>
