<h3 style="margin-top:2rem;color:#e6edf3">Scenario questions</h3>

<div class="scenario">
  <div class="sc-q">You want to add a Signal Generator block. It needs to remember its current frequency (default 1 kHz) and return it on FREQ? Which file do you create first, what class do you subclass, and what instance variable do you add?</div>
  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
  <div class="sc-ans">First file: silmulator/devices/signal_generator.py — create class SignalGenerator(BaseDevice). Instance variable: self._freq_hz: float = 1000.0 (set BEFORE super().__init__()). Register "FREQ" in _setup_handlers(). Handler: if cmd.is_query → return f"{self._freq_hz:.6f}"; if cmd.params → self._freq_hz = float(cmd.params[0]); return "". Then create the block (silmulator/gui/blocks/signal_generator_block.py) and register in canvas.py. Device first, because it has no GUI dependencies and can be tested standalone.</div>
</div>

<div class="scenario">
  <div class="sc-q">A colleague adds their <code>TemperatureLogger</code> device. The device class sets <code>self._temps = []</code> after calling <code>super().__init__()</code>. They report that everything works in normal use but <code>*RST</code> crashes the device. Diagnose the bug and give the fix.</div>
  <button class="rev-btn" onclick="rev(this)">Show answer</button>
  <div class="sc-ans">Bug: _do_reset() is called by BaseDevice.reset() (triggered by *RST). If _do_reset() tries to clear or replace self._temps, it executes successfully the first time — but only because the attribute was set in __init__ before the crash. Wait: actually if self._temps is set AFTER super().__init__(), it never exists when _setup_handlers() or _do_reset() is first called from super().__init__(). The *RST crash occurs because _do_reset() references self._temps but super().__init__() called it before self._temps existed. Fix: move self._temps = [] to BEFORE super().__init__(). Pattern: ALL instance variables must be set before super().__init__() in every BaseDevice subclass.</div>
</div>

<div class="scenario">
  <div class="sc-q">You want to write a unit test for your <code>MyDevice</code> class (not the block). What import do you need, how do you call process(), and what do you assert?</div>
